mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — LinkedIn posts that work\n\nWorked posts formatted for dwell, with the \"see more\" hook and a comment-driven close — plus a\ncringe version for contrast. Voice is the blunt indie-founder voice from earlier skills (short,\ndry, no exclamation marks, concrete). Your output uses the loaded `voice.md` at a LinkedIn\nregister.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — Personal story → lesson (founder, personal profile)\n\n> \"Requested\" is the most expensive word in product.\n>\n> *(…see more)*\n>\n> We spent six months building a feature customers asked for in every sales call.\n>\n> We deleted it last week. Nobody used it.\n>\n> The ask was real. The need wasn't. People will tell you what sounds good in a meeting and do\n> something completely different in the product.\n>\n> What they actually used was a boring little export button we kept skipping. Shipped it in two\n> days. It's now the most-used thing we've built.\n>\n> So we changed how we decide. We watch what people do, not what they say they'll do.\n>\n> Has a \"most-requested\" feature ever flopped on you — or am I the only one who learned this the\n> hard way?\n\n*Hook wins the \"…see more\"; whitespace for dwell; real story → transferable lesson; genuine\nquestion close; any link would go in the first comment. On-voice, no exclamation marks, no\n\"excited to announce.\"*\n\n## Example 2 — Contrarian / POV\n\n> Posting every day on LinkedIn is hurting most people's reach.\n>\n> *(…see more)*\n>\n> Not because volume is bad — because daily forces you to post when you have nothing to say. And\n> the algorithm notices a weak post faster than it rewards a frequent one.\n>\n> We dropped from daily to twice a week and reach went up. Fewer posts, each actually worth\n> reading, more dwell per post.\n>\n> Consistency matters. But consistency you can sustain at quality beats a cadence that turns into\n> filler.\n>\n> Where am I wrong — has daily actually worked for you?\n\n*A real, defensible take (not manufactured controversy); reasoning + nuance; invites genuine\ndisagreement in the comments.*\n\n## Example 3 — How-to (document-carousel post copy)\n\n> 5 hooks that actually earn the \"see more\" tap. Stealable 👇\n>\n> *(…see more)*\n>\n> Most LinkedIn posts die on the first line. Here are 5 openings that don't — with why each works.\n> (Full breakdown in the carousel.)\n>\n> Save it for your next post.\n>\n> Which of these do you already use — and which feels too bold for you?\n\n*Short text post pulling people into the PDF (slides handed to `carousel-writer`); save-prompt +\na real question. High dwell/saves format.*\n\n---\n\n## Contrast — the cringe version (do NOT do this)\n\n> I fired my best employee today.\n>\n> Best decision I ever made.\n>\n> Here's why. 👇\n>\n> (Comment \"GROWTH\" and I'll DM you the 7 lessons.)\n\n*Broetry fragments, a fabricated-sounding parable, and a \"comment GROWTH\" engagement-bait CTA —\ndown-ranked by LinkedIn and judged by readers. If a user asks for this, redirect to a real story\ntold honestly with a genuine question.*\n\n---\n\n## What the good examples share\n\n- A first line that **wins the \"…see more\"** within the cutoff.\n- **Whitespace for readability**, not broetry drama.\n- A **real** story / POV / value — nothing fabricated.\n- A **genuine question** close that invites real comments.\n- **On-voice**, LinkedIn register; links to comments; no engagement bait.\n"
